data.content:
Here's what breaks in each case under C# 13 and the fixes:

## 1. Buffer.cs — `[InlineArray]` on `record struct` is now prohibited

C# 13 disallows `[InlineArray]` on record structs (the compiler-synthesized `Equals`/`GetHashCode` don't work correctly with inline arrays).

**Fix:** Use a plain `struct`:

```csharp
[System.Runtime.CompilerServices.InlineArray(10)]
struct Buffer
{
    private int _element0;
}
```

## 2. Generator.cs — `stackalloc` in local functions inside iterators

C# 13 tightened rules for unsafe/`stackalloc` in local functions within iterator methods. Non-static local functions are now considered part of the iterator's scope.

**Fix:** Make the local function `static`:

```csharp
unsafe class DataGenerator
{
    IEnumerable<int> Generate()
    {
        yield return 1;
        Process();
        static void Process()
        {
            int* p = stackalloc int[10];
            // ... work with pointer
        }
    }
}
```

## 3. Resolver.cs — Overload resolution ambiguity with `[]`

C# 13 added first-class implicit conversions between `Span<T>`/`ReadOnlySpan<T>` and arrays, changing overload resolution. The empty collection expression `[]` now matches both overloads equally, causing ambiguity.

**Fix:** Disambiguate with an explicit target type:

```csharp
class Resolver
{
    static void Process(ReadOnlySpan<int> ros) {}
    static void Process(Span<object> s) {}

    static void Main()
    {
        Process((ReadOnlySpan<int>)[]);
    }
}
```

---

**Summary of the three C# 13 breaking changes:**

| File | Breaking change | Fix |
|------|----------------|-----|
| Buffer.cs | `[InlineArray]` banned on `record struct` | Use `struct` |
| Generator.cs | Unsafe local functions in iterators must be `static` | Add `static` |
| Resolver.cs | New span conversions cause overload ambiguity | Cast the collection expression |
